Common Principles that helps us(developers) to write clean, maintainable and efficient code :-

- KISS(Keep It Simple, Stupid):
    - We neither want less nor more, we only want to have as much as is required.
    - The main purpose is to reduce the complexity of the code which was written not to create a new complexity.
  
- DRY(Don't Repeat Yourself):
  - Main goal is to reduce the repetition of the code so that we can reduce the lines, can reduce the confusion.
  - So make sure you create common functions to avoid the repetition in your code.
  
- YAGNI(You Aren't Gonna Need It):
  - Don't implement anything until it is necessary because we are not gonna keep the code forever

- SOLID: helps in enhancing loose coupling
  - (S)ingle Responsibility Principle:
      -  A Class should have only one reason to change
      -  More like "Every class have a single job according to the requirements"
  - (O)pen-Closed Principle:
    -  Entities should be open for extension and closed for modifications
    -  More like "We can able to extend a class behavior without any modifying the code"
  - (L)iskov's Substitution Principle:
    - Child classes must be able to replace their parent classes
    - More like "Any subclass can be used in place of its parent class without causing unexpected behavior in the program"
  - (I)nterface Segregation Principle:
    - Keeping the interface details well-defined and avoid unnecessary dependencies
  - (D)ependency Inversion Principle:
    - High-level modules shouldn't depend on low-level modules while both depends on abstractions
    - The classes should always relay on abstractions rather than concrete implementations

- Seperation of Concerns(Soc):
  - More like making partitioning complicated application into different sections while each pointing out to specific jobs each
  - We can easily maintain, update and reuses the code

- Avoid Premature Optimization:
  - Don't do uneccessary optimisation at early stage of the development
  - Always do optimization when it is required at the right time with right benifits

- Law of Demeter:
  - This divides the responsibility between classes in three points
    - Each unit should have only limited knowledge about other units
    - Each unit should only talk to its friends, Don't talk to strangers
    - Only talk to your immediate friends



